{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"hallowed-usable-squeaker","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# S7 — Let the bot container reach Bedrock: IMDS hop limit 2, scoped IAM, documented exception\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe review-bot container cannot authenticate to Bedrock today. `infra/terraform/main.tf:50-54` sets\n`http_tokens = \"required\"` but leaves `http_put_response_hop_limit` unset, so it defaults to **1** — and a\nDocker container is one additional network hop, so IMDS is unreachable from inside the container. This matches\nthe note already in `pyproject.toml` that \"in-container IMDS is unreachable\" for the reviewbot's CloudWatch\npath. The instance role `rebar-gerrit-instance-role` (`infra/terraform/iam.tf:59`) also carries no Bedrock\npermission.\n\n## Approach\n\nSet `http_put_response_hop_limit = 2` in the instance's `metadata_options`, keeping `http_tokens = \"required\"`,\nand add a scoped `bedrock:InvokeModel` / `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` statement to the instance role\nrestricted to the Claude model and inference-profile ARNs the bot uses in `us-east-1`. This preserves ADR 0008's\ncentral win — no static AWS keys on disk, credentials short-lived and rotated by the role — and adds no new\nsecret to SSM.\n\nRaising the hop limit is a real, documented tradeoff: any container on the host can then reach the node role.\nAWS guidance is to treat hop limit > 1 as an exception that is recorded in a risk register and paired with\nnetwork-level containment, so this story does both — it adds the exception to the security posture record in\n`docs/adr/0046-security-posture-and-accepted-limitations.md` and blocks `169.254.169.254` from every container\nnetwork on the box except the review-bot's, so the widened reach is scoped to the one container that needs it.\n\n**Alternative rejected: a long-term Bedrock API key in SSM.** It fits the existing `fetch-secrets.sh` pattern\nwith no infra change, but AWS's own guidance restricts long-term Bedrock API keys to development and\nexperimentation and recommends roles with short-lived credentials for production; Wiz documented these keys\nappearing in public repositories within two weeks of launch. Trading short-lived role credentials for a\nlong-lived bearer token is the wrong direction for a box that already has a working instance role.\n\n**Alternative rejected: a host-side credential broker** minting short-lived credentials into the container. It\nkeeps hop limit at 1 but adds a bespoke component to build, operate, and secure for a benefit the iptables\ncontainment already provides.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `infra/terraform/main.tf` — `metadata_options.http_put_response_hop_limit = 2` (`:50-54`).\n- `infra/terraform/iam.tf` — scoped Bedrock statement on the instance role.\n- `infra/scripts/` — the container-network egress rule blocking `169.254.169.254` for non-bot networks.\n- `docs/adr/0046-security-posture-and-accepted-limitations.md` — the recorded exception.\n- `infra/runbooks/` — verification steps.\n\n## Testing\n\nReal-infrastructure tier: this is IAM and network reachability, so the oracle must be an actual call from inside\nthe running container, not a plan diff.\n\n- happy path: from inside the review-bot container, an IMDSv2 token PUT succeeds and `aws sts get-caller-identity`\n  returns the instance role; a minimal `bedrock-runtime` Converse call to the configured model returns 200.\n- negative control (the containment this story promises): the same IMDSv2 token PUT from a container on a\n  non-bot network **fails/times out**, proving the widened hop limit did not hand the node role to every container.\n- failure path: a Converse call for a model ARN outside the scoped statement is denied with `AccessDeniedException`,\n  proving least privilege rather than a blanket `bedrock:*`.\n- `terraform plan` is clean after apply (no drift).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `aws ec2 describe-instances` shows `HttpPutResponseHopLimit == 2` and `HttpTokens == required` on the host\n- [ ] a Bedrock Converse call from inside the review-bot container succeeds using instance-role credentials, with\n      no AWS key present in the container environment (evidenced by command output in a ticket comment)\n- [ ] the same IMDS request from a container on a non-bot network is blocked, evidenced in a ticket comment\n- [ ] a call to an out-of-scope model ARN returns `AccessDeniedException`\n- [ ] the hop-limit exception is recorded in `docs/adr/0046-security-posture-and-accepted-limitations.md` with its\n      containment control","id":"9249-1569-7be9-4f67","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S7 — let the bot container reach Bedrock: IMDS hop limit 2, scoped IAM, documented exception"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785008598278153001,"uuid":"b99fbd89-91ab-4c65-8bc6-a66a69b97d8a"}